{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22755","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22755","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22755","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhay","familyName":"Sheshadri","name":"Sheshadri, Abhay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aidan","familyName":"Ewart","name":"Ewart, Aidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elias","familyName":"Kempf","name":"Kempf, Elias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Fronsdal","name":"Fronsdal, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isha","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Isha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Schrodi","name":"Schrodi, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arthur","familyName":"Conmy","name":"Conmy, Arthur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel R.","familyName":"Bowman","name":"Bowman, Samuel R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Price","name":"Price, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Marks","name":"Marks, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rowan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Rowan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AuditBench: Evaluating Alignment Auditing Techniques on Models with Hidden Behaviors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T08:43:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:32:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T06:42:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:29:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-09T18:35:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-11T00:04:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-01T00:44:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-02T00:30:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce AuditBench, an alignment auditing benchmark. AuditBench consists of 56 language models with implanted hidden behaviors. Each model has one of 14 concerning behaviors--such as sycophantic deference, opposition to AI regulation, or secret geopolitical loyalties--which it does not confess to when directly asked. AuditBench models are highly diverse--some are subtle, while others are overt, and we use varying training techniques both for implanting behaviors and training models not to confess. To demonstrate AuditBench's utility, we develop an investigator agent that autonomously employs a configurable set of auditing tools. By measuring investigator agent success using different tools, we can evaluate their efficacy. Notably, we observe a tool-to-agent gap, where tools that perform well in standalone non-agentic evaluations fail to translate into improved performance when used with our investigator agent. We find that our most effective tools involve scaffolded calls to auxiliary models that generate diverse prompts for the target. White-box interpretability tools can be helpful, but the agent performs best with black-box tools. We also find that audit success varies greatly across training techniques: models trained on synthetic documents are easier to audit than models trained on demonstrations, with better adversarial training further increasing auditing difficulty. We release our models, agent, and evaluation framework to support future quantitative, iterative science on alignment auditing."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22755","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":2,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T02:53:52Z","registered":"2026-02-27T02:53:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-02T03:30:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23321","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23321","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.23321","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arsène","familyName":"Ferrière","name":"Ferrière, Arsène","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aurélien","familyName":"Benoit-Lévy","name":"Benoit-Lévy, Aurélien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olivier","familyName":"Martineau-Huynh","name":"Martineau-Huynh, Olivier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matías","familyName":"Tueros","name":"Tueros, Matías","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reconstruction of cosmic-ray direction and energy in radio arrays using deep ensemble graph neural networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T18:29:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T02:02:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-30T10:06:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-01T01:10:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Using advanced machine learning techniques, we developed a method to reconstruct the arrival direction and energy of ultra-high-energy cosmic rays from the voltage traces they induce on ground-based radio detector arrays.\n In our approach, triggered antennas are represented as a graph structure, which serves as input for a graph neural network (GNN). By incorporating physical knowledge into both the GNN architecture and the input data, we improve the precision and reduce the required size of the training set with respect to a fully data-driven approach. This method achieves an angular resolution of 0.092 degrees and an electromagnetic energy reconstruction resolution of 16.4% on simulated data with realistic noise conditions.\n We also employ uncertainty estimation methods to enhance the reliability of our predictions, quantifying the confidence of the GNN's outputs and providing confidence intervals for both direction and energy reconstruction. Finally, we investigate strategies to verify the model's consistency and robustness under real-life variations, with the goal of identifying scenarios in which predictions remain reliable despite domain shifts between simulation and reality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to Journal of Cosmology and Astroparticle Physics"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23321","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T03:07:59Z","registered":"2026-02-27T03:07:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-01T03:49:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22691","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22691","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22691","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kui","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Kui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cunhua","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Cunhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Mei","name":"Mei, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanting","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Wanting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunguo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chunguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"U-Net-Based Generative Joint Source-Channel Coding for Wireless Image Transmission"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T07:10:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:29:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-21T08:47:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-24T00:29:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-30T03:48:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-01T00:41:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep learning (DL)-based joint source-channel coding (JSCC) methods have achieved remarkable success in wireless image transmission. However, these methods either focus on conventional distortion metrics that do not necessarily yield high perceptual quality or incur high computational complexity. In this paper, we propose two DL-based JSCC (DeepJSCC) methods that leverage deep generative architectures for wireless image transmission. Specifically, we propose G-UNet-JSCC, a scheme comprising an encoder and a U-Net-based generator serving as the decoder. Its skip connections enable multi-scale feature fusion to improve both pixel-level fidelity and perceptual quality of reconstructed images by integrating low- and high-level features. To further enhance pixel-level fidelity, the encoder and the U-Net-based decoder are jointly optimized using a weighted sum of structural similarity and mean-squared error (MSE) losses. Building upon G-UNet-JSCC, we further develop a DeepJSCC method called cGAN-JSCC, where the decoder is enhanced through adversarial training. In this scheme, we retain the encoder of G-UNet-JSCC and adversarially train the decoder's generator against a patch-based discriminator. cGAN-JSCC employs a two-stage training procedure. The outer stage trains the encoder and the decoder end-to-end using an MSE loss, while the inner stage adversarially trains the decoder's generator and the discriminator by minimizing a joint loss combining adversarial and distortion losses. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed methods achieve superior pixel-level fidelity and perceptual quality on both high- and low-resolution images. For low-resolution images, cGAN-JSCC achieves better reconstruction performance and greater robustness to channel variations than G-UNet-JSCC."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This work has been submitted to the IEEE for possible publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22691","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T02:52:21Z","registered":"2026-02-27T02:52:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-01T03:49:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23257","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23257","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.23257","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jizhou","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Randomization Tests in Switchback Experiments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Econometrics (econ.EM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T17:30:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:59:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-29T03:40:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-30T00:39:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Switchback experiments assign an experimental unit, such as a market or a platform, to treatment or control over successive blocks of time. Inference can be challenging because these experiments often contain only a small number of randomized blocks, while outcomes may exhibit serial dependence, seasonality, and treatment effects that persist across periods. We develop a conditional randomization test for the null of no total treatment effect that is finite-sample valid under standard assumptions on temporal interference without requiring a parametric model for outcomes. We also develop randomization tests for these assumptions: a carryover test that assesses whether past assignments continue to affect outcomes beyond a prespecified horizon and a non-anticipation test that assesses whether future assignments affect current outcomes. For hypotheses about average treatment effects, we establish asymptotic validity of studentized randomization tests under additional regularity conditions. Finally, we derive power approximations and characterize the tradeoffs between experimental design choices, the number of informative randomized comparisons, and statistical power. Numerical experiments with stylized potential outcomes and a dynamic rideshare model illustrate finite-sample performance and implications for experimental design."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23257","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T03:06:25Z","registered":"2026-02-27T03:06:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-30T04:03:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23313","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23313","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.23313","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joonwon","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Joonwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kartik Anand","familyName":"Pant","name":"Pant, Kartik Anand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngim","familyName":"Nam","name":"Nam, Youngim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Hellmann","name":"Hellmann, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik","familyName":"Nune","name":"Nune, Karthik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Inseok","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Inseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Signal Temporal Logic Evaluation and Synthesis Using Deep Reachability Analysis and Layered Control Architecture"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T18:21:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T02:02:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-28T12:25:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-29T02:52:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a signal temporal logic (STL)-based framework that checks the feasibility of a mission described in STL and synthesizes control to safely execute it. The proposed framework evaluates the safety and reliability of operation through two phases. First, the proposed framework assesses the feasibility of STL by computing a backward reachable tube (BRT), which captures all states that can satisfy the given STL. The proposed framework accommodates the multiple reach-avoid (MRA) problem to address more general STL specifications and leverages a deep neural network to alleviate the computation burden for reachability analysis, significantly reducing the inference time compared to a baseline method. We further propose a layered planning and control architecture that combines mixed-integer linear programming (MILP) for global planning with model predictive control (MPC) as a local controller for the given STL. Consequently, the proposed framework can robustly handle unexpected behavior of obstacles that are not described in the environment information or STL, thereby providing reliable mission performance. Our numerical simulations demonstrate that the proposed framework can successfully compute BRT for a given STL and perform the mission."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23313","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T03:07:47Z","registered":"2026-02-27T03:07:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T07:01:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23258","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23258","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.23258","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuebo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xuebo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenkang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wenkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Miao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MASRubric: Auditing Information Flow in Multi-Agent Systems with Failure-Distilled Pitfall Rubrics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T17:31:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:59:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-28T17:34:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-29T01:25:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-28T14:15:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-29T03:03:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While multi-agent systems (MAS) excel at complex reasoning, they are vulnerable to errors that intermediate agents introduce and downstream agents build upon. Auditing intermediate messages before they propagate requires an explicit standard, yet evaluation rubrics are typically authored by domain experts or written against a reference answer, neither of which is available for an unseen message at test time. We present MASRubric, a MAS information flow auditing framework with failure-distilled pitfall rubrics. Offline, trajectories on which the MAS has failed are automatically distilled into a reusable bank of pitfall criteria, each describing a recurrent error by its underlying misconception, the reasoning situations in which it arises, and the check that would expose it. Online, the criteria applicable to each intermediate message are retrieved from this off-the-shelf bank and checked one by one, and the resulting satisfaction rate decides whether the message is broadcast, returned to its author with diagnostic feedback for revision, or withheld. Empirical results demonstrate that MASRubric enhances MAS performance on both fixed and dynamic frameworks, achieving average accuracy gains of up to 2.83 points on math reasoning benchmarks and 1.74 points on code generation benchmarks. Further analysis shows that the retrieved criteria vary systematically with task types, and that the audit effort tracks task difficulty. Moreover, the bank transfers without re-mining to a system with a stronger backbone, which makes more adaptive and more efficient use of it. Our code and dataset are released at https://github.com/TonySY2/MASRubric."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23258","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T03:06:26Z","registered":"2026-02-27T03:06:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T07:01:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23024","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23024","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.23024","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cui","familyName":"Wenbo","name":"Wenbo, Cui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongpu","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Zhongpu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongliang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongbin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Dongbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InCoM: Intent-Driven Perception and Structured Coordination for Mobile Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T14:03:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:47:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-27T12:00:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-28T01:37:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-28T06:27:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-29T00:28:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-29T09:32:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:37:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-13T07:47:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-14T01:13:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-21T12:50:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-07-22T00:48:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-09-28T12:06:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2026-09-29T02:50:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"7","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mobile manipulation is a fundamental capability for general-purpose robotic agents, requiring both coordinated control of the mobile base and manipulator and robust perception under dynamically changing viewpoints. However, existing approaches face two key challenges: strong coupling between base and arm actions complicates control optimization, and perceptual attention is often poorly allocated as viewpoints shift during mobile manipulation. We propose InCoM, an intent-driven perception and structured coordination framework for mobile manipulation. InCoM infers latent motion intent to dynamically reweight multi-scale perceptual features, enabling stage-adaptive allocation of perceptual attention. To support robust cross-modal perception, InCoM further incorporates a geometric-semantic affinity refinement mechanism that enhances multimodal correspondence. On the control side, we design a decoupled coordinated flow matching action decoder that explicitly models bidirectional base-arm interactions, alleviating optimization difficulties caused by control coupling. Under matched non-privileged observations and task-specific training data, InCoM improves mean success over DSPv2 by 33.0, 26.1, and 23.6 percentage points on SetTable, TidyHouse, and PrepareGroceries, respectively. Furthermore, its effectiveness is consistently validated in real-world mobile manipulation tasks, where InCoM maintains a superior success rate over existing baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The project website is available at https://liujiahao2077.github.io/InCoM.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23024","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T03:00:37Z","registered":"2026-02-27T03:00:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T07:01:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22453","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22453","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22453","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaswat","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Shaswat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishvesh","familyName":"Trivedi","name":"Trivedi, Vishvesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihuai","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Yihuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eunsol","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Eunsol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Identifying Crucial Attention Heads for Multilingual Language Models: Retrieval and Retrieval-Transition Heads"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T22:28:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:10:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T17:36:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:58:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T01:26:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-03T00:18:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-11T19:22:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-15T00:10:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval heads, a subset of attention heads in Transformers, were studied in English, showing its crucial role in retrieving information from the context. We expand the study of retrieval heads to multilingual context and find that while nearly half of all retrieval heads are often shared across multiple languages, language-specific retrieval heads also emerge. We further design a cross-lingual needle-in-the-haystack task, to identify $\\textit{Retrieval Transition Heads (RTH)}$ that retrieve key information and further map it to target-language output. Our experiments reveal that RTHs do not always overlap with RH, and can be more vital for Chain-of-Thought reasoning in multilingual LLMs. Across four multilingual benchmarks (MMLU-ProX, MGSM, MLQA, and XQuAD) and two model families (Qwen2.5-7B Instruct and Llama3.1-8B Instruct), we demonstrate that masking RTH induces bigger performance drop than masking Retrieval Heads (RH). Specifically, for Llama3.1-8B Instruct, masking the top-25 RTHs results in an average 37.3-point drop (against 19.5-point drop from RH masking) in reasoning accuracy (MMLU-ProX, MGSM) and 9.0 F1-score reduction (against 5.1 drop for RH) in extractive QA (MLQA, XQuAD). Our work advances the understanding of multilingual LMs by isolating the attention heads responsible for mapping to target languages."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22453","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T02:46:43Z","registered":"2026-02-27T02:46:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-15T04:45:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22787","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22787","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22787","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivo","familyName":"Brink","name":"Brink, Ivo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Boer","name":"Boer, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dennis","familyName":"Ulmer","name":"Ulmer, Dennis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Probing for Knowledge Attribution in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T09:21:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:34:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-27T16:19:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-28T01:20:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-10T12:57:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-11T00:50:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) hallucinations, meaning fluent but factually incorrect generations, fall into two types: faithfulness violations, where the model misuses provided context, and factuality violations, where answers reflect errors in internal knowledge. Proper mitigation depends on knowing which source drives each answer. We study contributive attribution, i.e. the classification of the dominant knowledge source behind each output, and show that a simple linear probe trained on hidden representations can reliably identify it. We introduce AttriWiki, a self-supervised pipeline that automatically generates labelled training data by prompting models to recall withheld entities from memory or read them from context without relying on knowledge conflicts. Probes trained on AttriWiki achieve up to 0.96 Macro-$F_1$ on Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B, and Qwen-7B, transfer to SQuAD and WebQuestions with 0.94-0.99 Macro-$F_1$, and generalise zero-shot to Tighidet et al. (2024)'s benchmark, outperforming their probe on conflicting settings without retraining. Furthermore, attribution mismatches raise error rates by up to 70%, though correct attribution does not guarantee correct answers, pointing to the need for broader detection frameworks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22787","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":5,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T02:54:37Z","registered":"2026-02-27T02:54:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-11T02:38:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22766","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22766","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22766","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"You","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, You","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanghao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yanghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanhu","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Fanhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyu","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kaiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maosong","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Maosong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Imagination Helps Visual Reasoning, But Not Yet in Latent Space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T08:56:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:33:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-07T11:29:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-09T00:58:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-03T12:22:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-04T00:49:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Latent visual reasoning aims to mimic human's imagination process by meditating through hidden states of Multimodal Large Language Models. While recognized as a promising paradigm for visual reasoning, the underlying mechanisms driving its effectiveness remain unclear. Motivated to demystify the true source of its efficacy, we investigate the validity of latent reasoning using Causal Mediation Analysis. We model the process as a causal chain: the input as the treatment, the latent tokens as the mediator, and the final answer as the outcome. Our findings uncover two critical disconnections: (a) Input-Latent Disconnect: dramatic perturbations on the input result in negligible changes to the latent tokens, suggesting that latent tokens do not effectively attend to the input sequence. (b) Latent-Answer Disconnect: perturbations on the latent tokens yield minimal impact on the final answer, indicating the limited causal effect latent tokens imposing on the outcome. Furthermore, extensive probing analysis reveals that latent tokens encode limited visual information and exhibit high similarity. Consequently, we challenge the necessity of latent reasoning and propose a straightforward alternative named CapImagine, which teaches the model to explicitly imagine using text. Experiments on vision-centric benchmarks show that CapImagine significantly outperforms complex latent-space baselines, highlighting the superior potential of visual reasoning through explicit imagination."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026 Poster"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22766","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T02:54:07Z","registered":"2026-02-27T02:54:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-04T02:40:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23188","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23188","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.23188","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ismaël","familyName":"Zighed","name":"Zighed, Ismaël","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Nóvoa","name":"Nóvoa, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Magri","name":"Magri, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taraneh","familyName":"Sayadi","name":"Sayadi, Taraneh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Adaptation of ROMs for Unsteady Flows Using Data Assimilation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T16:43:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:56:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-31T13:32:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T02:07:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T09:45:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-02T00:51:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.compfluid.2026.107250.","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose an efficient retraining strategy for a parameterized Reduced Order Model (ROM) that attains accuracy comparable to full retraining while requiring only a fraction of the computational time and relying solely on sparse observations of the full system. The architecture employs an encode-process-decode structure: a Variational Autoencoder (VAE) to perform dimensionality reduction, and a transformer network to evolve the latent states and model the dynamics. The ROM is parameterized by an external control variable, the Reynolds number in the Navier-Stokes setting, with the transformer exploiting attention mechanisms to capture both temporal dependencies and parameter effects. The probabilistic VAE enables stochastic sampling of trajectory ensembles, providing predictive means and uncertainty quantification through the first two moments. After initial training on a limited set of dynamical regimes, the model is adapted to out-of-sample parameter regions using only sparse data. Its probabilistic formulation naturally supports ensemble generation, which we employ within an ensemble Kalman filtering framework to assimilate data and reconstruct full-state trajectories from minimal observations. We further show that, for the dynamical system considered, the dominant source of error in out-of-sample forecasts stems from distortions of the latent manifold rather than changes in the latent dynamics. Consequently, retraining can be limited to the autoencoder, allowing for a lightweight, computationally efficient adaptation procedure with very sparse fine-tuning data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23188","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-27T03:04:37Z","registered":"2026-02-27T03:04:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T03:03:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23088","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23088","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.23088","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Sutton","name":"Sutton, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katrin","familyName":"Amunts","name":"Amunts, Katrin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timo","familyName":"Dickscheid","name":"Dickscheid, Timo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Schiffer","name":"Schiffer, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cytoarchitecture in Words: Weakly Supervised Vision-Language Modeling for Human Brain Microscopy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.7; I.4.9; I.5.1; I.5.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T15:10:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:51:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T12:01:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:49:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision foundation models increasingly support interactive scientific workflows, but natural-language interaction requires coupling visual representations to language. Curated image-text pairs for this coupling are scarce in many biomedical domains. This is the case for cell-body-stained histological sections of the human brain, where microscopic image patches encode cytoarchitecture: cellular density, morphology, laminar structure, and areal organization. We propose retrieve-and-enrich supervision, a weakly supervised scheme for training image-conditioned language models without curated image-text pairs. The method retrieves label-level text from the literature via a shared anatomical label, then enriches it with image-specific properties such as cortical layer thickness and cell density. Labels provide training targets only, not model inputs. We use this scheme to couple CytoNet, a cytoarchitectonic vision foundation model, to an open-weight large language model via a lightweight Flamingo-style adapter. Across 57 brain areas, the resulting model produces plausible cytoarchitectonic descriptions, supports open-set use by rejecting out-of-scope areas, and predicts the correct area for in-scope patches with 90.6% accuracy. Removing explicit area names from generated text still leaves descriptions sufficient for a language model to recover the area in an 8-way test with 68.6% accuracy. A second instruction-tuned model trained with image-specific targets recovers cortical layer thickness and cell-densities from individual patches, allowing the model to read out information beyond canonical area descriptions. Our results show that retrieve-and-enrich supervision offers a practical route to vision-language training in specialized imaging domains where labels and expert text exist but image-level captions do not."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 5 figures, accepted for inclusion at GCPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23088","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T03:02:09Z","registered":"2026-02-27T03:02:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:26:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22456","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22456","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22456","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ikram","familyName":"Darif","name":"Darif, Ikram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feifei","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Feifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manel","familyName":"Abdellatif","name":"Abdellatif, Manel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lionel C.","familyName":"Briand","name":"Briand, Lionel C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramesh","familyName":"S.","name":"S., Ramesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arun","familyName":"Adiththan","name":"Adiththan, Arun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automating the Detection of Requirement Dependencies Using Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T22:33:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:10:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-14T22:30:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:11:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Requirements are inherently interconnected through various types of dependencies. Identifying these dependencies is essential, as they underpin critical decisions and influence a range of activities throughout software development. However, this task is challenging, particularly in modern software systems, given the high volume of complex, coupled requirements. These challenges are further exacerbated by the ambiguity of Natural Language (NL) requirements and their constant change. Consequently, requirement dependency detection is often overlooked or performed manually. Large Language Models (LLMs) exhibit strong capabilities in NL processing, presenting a promising avenue for requirement-related tasks. While they have shown to enhance various requirements engineering tasks, their effectiveness in identifying requirement dependencies remains unexplored. In this paper, we introduce LEREDD, an LLM-based approach for automated detection of requirement dependencies that leverages Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and In-Context Learning (ICL). It is designed to identify diverse dependency types directly from NL requirements. We empirically evaluate LEREDD against two state-of-the-art baselines. The results show that LEREDD provides highly accurate classification of dependent and non-dependent requirements, achieving an accuracy of 0.93, and an F1 score of 0.84, with the latter averaging 0.96 for non-dependent cases. LEREDD outperforms zero-shot LLMs and baselines, particularly in detecting fine-grained dependency types, where it yields average relative gains of 94.87% and 105.41% in F1 scores for the Requires dependency over the baselines. We also provide an annotated dataset of requirement dependencies encompassing 813 requirement pairs across three distinct systems to support reproducibility and future research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22456","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T02:46:47Z","registered":"2026-02-27T02:46:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T03:59:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22647","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22647","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22647","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyang","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zhengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isay","familyName":"Katsman","name":"Katsman, Isay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yueqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruining","familyName":"He","name":"He, Ruining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukasz","familyName":"Heldt","name":"Heldt, Lukasz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raghunandan","familyName":"Keshavan","name":"Keshavan, Raghunandan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shao-Chuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shao-Chuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyang","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Xinyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Mingyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Onkar","familyName":"Dalal","name":"Dalal, Onkar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lichan","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Lichan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ed","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Ed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningren","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Ningren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vectorizing the Trie: Efficient Constrained Decoding for LLM-based Generative Retrieval on Accelerators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T06:00:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:26:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-20T21:24:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-17T00:17:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3770855.3818506","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative retrieval has emerged as a powerful paradigm for LLM-based recommendation. However, industrial recommender systems often benefit from restricting the output space to a constrained subset of items based on business logic (e.g. enforcing content freshness or product category), which standard autoregressive decoding cannot natively support. Moreover, existing constrained decoding methods that make use of prefix trees (Tries) incur severe latency penalties on hardware accelerators (TPUs/GPUs). In this work, we introduce STATIC (Sparse Transition Matrix-Accelerated Trie Index for Constrained Decoding), an efficient and scalable constrained decoding technique designed specifically for high-throughput LLM-based generative retrieval on TPUs/GPUs. By flattening the prefix tree into a static Compressed Sparse Row (CSR) matrix, we transform irregular tree traversals into fully vectorized sparse matrix operations, unlocking massive efficiency gains on hardware accelerators. We deploy STATIC on a large-scale industrial video recommendation platform serving billions of users. STATIC produces significant product metric impact with minimal latency overhead (0.033 ms per step and 0.25% of inference time), achieving a 948x speedup over a CPU trie implementation and a 47-1033x speedup over a hardware-accelerated binary-search baseline. Furthermore, the runtime overhead of STATIC remains extremely low across a wide range of practical configurations. To the best of our knowledge, STATIC enables the first production-scale deployment of strictly constrained generative retrieval. In addition, evaluation on academic benchmarks demonstrates that STATIC can considerably improve cold-start performance for generative retrieval. Our code is available at https://github.com/youtube/static-constraint-decoding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"KDD 2026 camera-ready"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22647","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-27T02:51:18Z","registered":"2026-02-27T02:51:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-17T02:07:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22241","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22241","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22241","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bodo","familyName":"Rosenhahn","name":"Rosenhahn, Bodo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobias J.","familyName":"Osborne","name":"Osborne, Tobias J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Hirche","name":"Hirche, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stochastic Neural Networks for Quantum Devices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T10:16:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:00:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T17:24:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T01:04:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work presents a formulation to express and optimize stochastic neural networks as quantum circuits in gate-based quantum computing. Motivated by a classical perceptron, stochastic artificial neurons are introduced and combined into a quantum neural network. The Kiefer-Wolfowitz algorithm in combination with simulated annealing is used for training the network weights. Several topologies and models are presented, including shallow fully connected networks, Hopfield Networks, Restricted Boltzmann Machines, Autoencoders and convolutional neural networks. We also demonstrate the combination of our optimized neural networks as an oracle for the Grover algorithm to realize a quantum generative AI model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages; v2: 19 pages. Some extensions and clarifications"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22241","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T02:41:49Z","registered":"2026-02-27T02:41:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:24:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23035","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23035","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.23035","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Viraj","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Viraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marko","familyName":"Grujic","name":"Grujic, Marko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Aigner","name":"Aigner, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Theodor","familyName":"Abart","name":"Abart, Theodor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcus","familyName":"Granegger","name":"Granegger, Marcus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deblina","familyName":"Bhattacharjee","name":"Bhattacharjee, Deblina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katharine","familyName":"Fraser","name":"Fraser, Katharine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Disease-Sensitive Latent Interaction Graphs From Noisy Cardiac Flow Measurements"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T14:17:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:48:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T14:21:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:57:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cardiac blood flow patterns contain rich information about disease severity and clinical interventions, yet current imaging and computational methods fail to capture underlying relational structures of coherent flow features. We propose a physics-informed, latent relational framework to model cardiac vortices as interacting nodes in a graph. Our model combines a neural relational inference architecture with physics-inspired interaction energy and birth-death dynamics, yielding a latent graph sensitive to disease severity and intervention level. We first develop the method using computational fluid dynamics simulations of aortic coarctation, where learned interaction graphs reveal increasingly structured vortex interactions as vessel narrowing progresses. The resulting graph entropy exhibits a strong monotonic relationship with coarctation severity ($R^2=0.78$, Spearman $|ρ|=0.96$). We then evaluate the framework on fluid-structure interaction simulations of intracranial aneurysms using multiple geometric severity descriptors. Among these, aneurysm volume produces the most informative latent representation, with non-interaction graph entropy demonstrating a strong monotonic relationship with severity (Spearman $|ρ| = 0.91$) and generalising to several alternative morphological measures. Finally, we apply the approach to ultrasound-derived flow fields of a left ventricle under varying levels of left ventricular assist device support, where the latent graph captures the progressive loss of coherent vortex interactions under mechanical assistance, demonstrating cross-modal generalisation to imaging data. Across all datasets, latent interaction graphs and graph entropy provide interpretable markers of disease severity and intervention, linking haemodynamic organisation to clinically relevant physiological changes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23035","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T03:00:53Z","registered":"2026-02-27T03:00:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:34:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23290","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23290","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.23290","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyang","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Zhengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renzhi","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Renzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jenny","familyName":"Suckale","name":"Suckale, Jenny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LineGraph2Road: Structural Graph Reasoning on Line Graphs for Road Network Extraction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T18:02:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T02:00:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-08T19:57:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:45:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Extracting routable road networks from satellite imagery requires accurate topology recovery beyond pixel-level segmentation. Recent methods decompose the task into keypoint detection and connectivity prediction, but reliably inferring road connectivity under structural ambiguity remains challenging. Overpasses introduce non-planar crossings that can create false shortcuts, and occlusions break visual continuity, requiring long-range structural reasoning. We formulate road extraction as fully unobserved connectivity inference and construct a global but sparse Euclidean graph from detected keypoints to better leverage the long-term context. To improve link prediction, we transform this graph into its line graph and perform reasoning using a Graph Transformer. We propose an end-to-end pipeline that integrates vision-based segmentation, sparse graph construction, and structured inference. Our method explicitly models overpasses and uses topology-preserving vertex extraction to mitigate routing-critical errors. This approach achieves state-of-the-art performance on City-scale, SpaceNet, and Global-scale benchmarks in topology metrics including TOPO-F1 and APLS. In addition to quantitative gains, our method improves reconstruction of multi-level road structures crucial for real-world routing reliability. The code is available at: https://github.com/wzzzzzzy/LineGraph2Road."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23290","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T03:07:15Z","registered":"2026-02-27T03:07:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:17:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22895","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22895","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22895","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruno","familyName":"Aristimunha","name":"Aristimunha, Bruno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ce","familyName":"Ju","name":"Ju, Ce","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antoine","familyName":"Collas","name":"Collas, Antoine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florent","familyName":"Bouchard","name":"Bouchard, Florent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ammar","familyName":"Mian","name":"Mian, Ammar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bertrand","familyName":"Thirion","name":"Thirion, Bertrand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sylvain","familyName":"Chevallier","name":"Chevallier, Sylvain","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reinmar","familyName":"Kobler","name":"Kobler, Reinmar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SPD Learn: A Geometric Deep Learning Python Library for Neural Decoding Through Trivialization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T11:33:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:40:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-10T10:52:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:43:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Implementations of symmetric positive definite (SPD) matrix-based neural networks for neural decoding remain fragmented across research codebases and Python packages. Existing implementations often employ ad hoc handling of manifold constraints and non-unified training setups, which hinders reproducibility and integration into modern deep-learning workflows. To address this gap, we introduce SPD Learn, a unified and modular Python package for geometric deep learning with SPD matrices. SPD Learn provides core SPD operators and neural-network layers, including numerically stable spectral operators, and enforces Stiefel/SPD constraints via trivialization-based parameterizations. This design enables standard backpropagation and optimization in unconstrained Euclidean spaces while producing manifold-constrained parameters by construction. The package also offers reference implementations of representative SPDNet-based models and interfaces with widely used brain computer interface/neuroimaging toolkits and modern machine-learning libraries (e.g., MOABB, Braindecode, Nilearn, and SKADA), facilitating reproducible benchmarking and practical deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 Pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22895","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T02:57:22Z","registered":"2026-02-27T02:57:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:17:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22794","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22794","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22794","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soroosh","familyName":"Miri","name":"Miri, Soroosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sepehr","familyName":"Abolhasani","name":"Abolhasani, Sepehr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shahrokh","familyName":"Farahmand","name":"Farahmand, Shahrokh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S. Mohammad","familyName":"Razavizadeh","name":"Razavizadeh, S. Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiguang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Doubly Adaptive Channel and Spatial Attention for Semantic Image Communication by IoT Devices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T09:29:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:35:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-09T19:35:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:15:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Internet of Things (IoT) networks face significant challenges such as limited communication bandwidth, constrained computational and energy resources, and highly dynamic wireless channel conditions. Utilization of deep neural networks (DNNs) combined with semantic communication has emerged as a promising paradigm to address these limitations. Deep joint source-channel coding (DJSCC) has recently been proposed to enable semantic communication of images. Building upon the original DJSCC formulation, low-complexity attention-style architectures has been added to the DNNs for further performance enhancement. As a main hurdle, training these DNNs separately for various signal-to-noise ratios (SNRs) will amount to excessive storage or communication overhead, which can not be maintained by small IoT devices. SNR Adaptive DJSCC (ADJSCC), has been proposed to train the DNNs once but feed the current SNR as part of the data to the channel-wise attention mechanism. We improve upon ADJSCC by a simultaneous utilization of doubly adaptive channel-wise and spatial attention modules at both transmitter and receiver. These modules dynamically adjust to varying channel conditions and spatial feature importance, enabling robust and efficient feature extraction and semantic information recovery. Simulation results corroborate that our proposed doubly adaptive DJSCC (DA-DJSCC) significantly improves upon ADJSCC in several performance criteria, while incurring a mild increase in complexity. These facts render DA-DJSCC a desirable choice for semantic communication in performance demanding but low-complexity IoT networks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 7 figures, conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22794","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T02:54:48Z","registered":"2026-02-27T02:54:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:16:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22632","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22632","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22632","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leheng","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Leheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feifang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Feifang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang-Rong","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Xiang-Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiancan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiancan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangnan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xiangnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fine-grained Semantics Integration for Large Language Model-based Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T05:17:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:25:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-28T11:32:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:38:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-09T07:07:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:56:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) have driven a shift in recommender systems from the discriminative paradigm to the LLM-based generative paradigm, where the recommender autoregressively generates sequences of semantic identifiers (SIDs) for target items conditioned on historical interaction. While prevalent LLM-based recommenders have demonstrated performance gains by aligning pretrained LLMs between the language space and the SID space, modeling the SID space still faces two fundamental challenges: (i) Semantically Meaningless Initialization: SID tokens are initialized by random sampling without incorporating token-specific semantics, leaving them without explicit semantic correspondence to the pretrained language space; and (ii) Coarse-grained Alignment: existing SFT-based alignment tasks primarily focus on item-level optimization while overlooking the semantics of individual tokens within SID sequences. To address these challenges, we propose TS-Rec, which can integrate Token-level Semantics into LLM-based Recommenders. Specifically, TS-Rec comprises two key components: (1) Semantic-Aware Embedding Initialization (SA-Init), which initializes SID token embeddings by applying mean pooling to the pretrained embeddings of keywords extracted by a teacher model; and (2) Token-level Semantic Alignment (TS-Align), which aligns individual tokens within the SID sequence with the shared semantics of the corresponding item clusters. Extensive experiments on three real-world benchmarks demonstrate that TS-Rec consistently outperforms traditional and generative baselines across all standard metrics. These findings confirm the effectiveness of incorporating fine-grained semantic information into LLM-based generative recommenders. The implementation is available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/TS-Rec-Review-8F99/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22632","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T02:50:56Z","registered":"2026-02-27T02:50:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:16:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23050","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23050","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.23050","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexej","familyName":"Klushyn","name":"Klushyn, Alexej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Kurle","name":"Kurle, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maximilian","familyName":"Soelch","name":"Soelch, Maximilian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Botond","familyName":"Cseke","name":"Cseke, Botond","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"van der Smagt","name":"van der Smagt, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Latent Matters: Learning Deep State-Space Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T14:35:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:49:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-30T08:24:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-31T00:35:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep state-space models (DSSMs) enable temporal predictions by learning the underlying dynamics of observed sequence data. They are often trained by maximising the evidence lower bound. However, as we show, this does not ensure the model actually learns the underlying dynamics. We therefore propose a constrained optimisation framework as a general approach for training DSSMs. Building upon this, we introduce the extended Kalman VAE (EKVAE), which combines amortised variational inference with classic Bayesian filtering/smoothing to model dynamics more accurately than RNN-based DSSMs. Our results show that the constrained optimisation framework significantly improves system identification and prediction accuracy on the example of established state-of-the-art DSSMs. The EKVAE outperforms previous models w.r.t. prediction accuracy, achieves remarkable results in identifying dynamical systems, and can furthermore successfully learn state-space representations where static and dynamic features are disentangled."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at NeurIPS 2021"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23050","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T03:01:15Z","registered":"2026-02-27T03:01:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-31T02:37:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23297","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.23297","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.23297","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunming","familyName":"He","name":"He, Chunming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ziyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria","familyName":"Woodward","name":"Woodward, Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming-Chen","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Ming-Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mercy","familyName":"Pawar","name":"Pawar, Mercy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leslie","familyName":"Niziol","name":"Niziol, Leslie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sina","familyName":"Farsiu","name":"Farsiu, Sina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PRIMA: Pre-Training with Risk-Integrated Image--Metadata Alignment for Medical Diagnosis with LLM-Based Feature Aggregation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-26T18:07:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T02:01:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-04T00:47:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:03:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-28T06:47:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-29T00:29:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical diagnosis requires the effective synthesis of visual manifestations and clinical metadata. However, existing methods often treat metadata as isolated tags, failing to exploit the rich semantic knowledge embedded in clinical descriptions. We propose PRIMA (Pre-training with Risk-integrated Image-Metadata Alignment), a framework that integrates domain-specific knowledge into multi-modal representation learning. We first curate a corpus of risk--disease correlations via Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to refine Clinical ModernBERT, embedding diagnostic priors into the text encoder. To bridge the modality gap, we introduce a dual-encoder pre-training strategy utilizing DINOv3 and our refined Clinical ModernBERT, optimized by a suite of four complementary loss functions. These losses are designed to capture multi-granular semantic alignment and handle the ambiguity of clinical correlations through soft labels. Finally, we leverage Qwen3 to fuse these aligned features for precise disease classification. Extensive experiments demonstrate that PRIMA effectively harmonizes pixel-level features with abstract clinical expertise. Across PAD-UFES-20 and AQUA, PRIMA achieves average F1-scores of 72.04\\% and 85.22\\%, outperforming strong image-only, metadata-fusion, and medical vision-language baselines. Notably, our framework achieves strong performance without requiring massive data collection or exhaustive computational resources. Our code is available at https://github.com/yqwang01/PRIMA."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23297","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-27T03:07:25Z","registered":"2026-02-27T03:07:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-29T02:29:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22248","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22248","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22248","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Sunny"],"givenName":"Julia","familyName":"Gonski","name":"Gonski, Julia","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Sunny"],"givenName":"Jenni","familyName":"Ott","name":"Ott,